On Tue, 31 Oct 2000, SteveCraft wrote:

> Has anyone used Arkeia as their backup solution?

I evaluated it for my last job and was pretty impressed.  It's got a
decent CLI interface and a pretty nice GUI for those who weren't too
command-line savvy.  It was also a lot quicker than I thought it would be
over the network.

> I have my AXP Linux build here, but an NT server on the LAN with the tape
> drive.  Arkeia seems to make something that has an AXP Linux client that
> will talk to an NT server.

It appears so.  I tried it on a few Intel boxes before, so I didn't even
realise that they had come out with an Alpha client, but it should work
the same.  A word of warning, the CLI is not for the faint of heart and
can get a tad ugly if you're not too familiar with some of the terms that
they use (assuming that they haven't changed anything since I
tried it).  At any rate, I did the opposite of what you're doing...I used
a Linux backup server and connected with a Windows NT Arkeia client (it
was the only time I even remotely wanted to touch WinNT :-P).  It worked
great.

> I was originally trying to make a shell script that would make the Linux
> box tar to an FTP site on the NT box and then make NT back that up, but it
> sure is ugly.  It looks like Arkeia has something CLI that I can simply
> cron and let it run.

IIRC, it has it's own scheduler, so cron may not be needed.  You'll have
to check the docs, but they're not bad, so you should be able to find your
answer there.

In all, it's a good package for network backups, especially since I found
that it was usually cheaper than comparable "name-brand" software
(read: ARCserve), but with a similar list of features and good
performance.

C




